/* book: lead: Micro actuators make controlled motion on the scale of micrometres: a valve opens, a mirror tilts, a droplet leaves a nozzle. They exist because coils and magnets do not shrink well; a tiny coil has few turns and high resistance, so magnetic force falls away. Three common families use piezoelectric strain, shape-memory phase change, or electrostatic attraction. You meet them in inkjet print heads, */
/* book Figure 6.7.1: A 200 µm square plate 2 µm above a fixed electrode feels 39.8 µN at 30 V. The electric force rises as 1/(g₀ − x)², the spring force in a straight line; at the pull-in voltage they touch at x = g₀/3, so past 0.667 µm the plate snaps down. */
