/* book Worked example: Q: A parallel-plate electrostatic micro actuator has a square plate 200 µm × 200 µm, a gap of 2 µm and an applied voltage of 30 V. Take ε0 = 8.854 × 10−12 F/m. Find the capacitance, the electrostatic force, and the displacement at which pull-in occurs, together with the remaining gap at that point.
   1. Area A = (200 × 10−6 m)² = 4 × 10−8 m².
   2. Gap g = 2 × 10−6 m. Capacitance C = ε0A/g = (8.854 × 10−12)(4 × 10−8)/(2 × 10−6) = 1.7708 × 10−13 F = 0.177 pF.
   3. Voltage V = 30 V, so V² = 900 V² and g² = 4 × 10−12 m².
   4. Force F = ε0AV²/(2g²) = (8.854 × 10−12)(4 × 10−8)(900)/(2 × 4 × 10−12) = 3.984 × 10−5 N = 39.8 µN.
   5. Pull-in occurs when the plate has moved one third of the initial gap: displacement = g0/3 = (2 µm)/3 = 0.667 µm, so the remaining gap is 2g0/3 = 1.33 µm.
   Answer: Capacitance is about 0.177 pF, electrostatic force is about 39.8 µN, and pull-in occurs after 0.667 µm of travel, at a remaining gap of about 1.33 µm. */
