/* book Worked example 2: Q: (a) A Hay bridge balances a coil at 1 kHz with R₁ = 100 Ω in series with C₁ = 0.1 µF, R₂ = 1 kΩ and R₃ = 10 kΩ. Find Q, Lx and Rx, using Rx = ω²R₁R₂R₃C₁² / (1 + ω²R₁²C₁²). (b) A Schering bridge tests a cable at 50 Hz with C₂ = 100 pF, R₃ = 400 Ω, R₄ = 2000 Ω and C₄ = 0.016 µF. Find Cx and tan δ.
   1. (a) ω = 2π × 1000 = 6283 rad/s; ωR₁C₁ = 6283 × 100 × 0.1 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.06283, so Q = 1/(ωR₁C₁) = 15.9, high enough for a Hay bridge.
   2. Correction: ω²R₁²C₁² = 0.06283² = 0.003948, so 1 + 0.003948 = 1.003948.
   3. Lx = R₂R₃C₁ / 1.003948 = (1000 × 10 000 × 0.1 × 10⁻⁶) / 1.003948 = 1 / 1.003948 = 0.9961 H.
   4. Rx = ω²R₁R₂R₃C₁² / 1.003948 = (6283² × 100 × 1000 × 10 000 × 10⁻¹⁴) / 1.003948 = 394.8 / 1.003948 = 393.2 Ω. Check: ωLx/Rx = 6283 × 0.9961 / 393.2 = 15.9 = Q.
   5. (b) Cx = C₂R₄/R₃ = 100 pF × 2000 / 400 = 500 pF.
   6. tan δ = ωR₄C₄ = 2π × 50 × 2000 × 0.016 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.0101, a dissipation factor of about 1 %, typical of aged paper insulation; new polymer cable is far lower.
   Answer: Hay: Q = 15.9, Lx = 0.996 H, Rx = 393 Ω. Schering: Cx = 500 pF, tan δ = 0.0101. */
