/* book exam traps: Using a Wheatstone bridge below about 1 Ω: lead and contact resistance then add to the arm and the null is wrong; use the Kelvin double bridge with four terminals. | Mixing bridge and quantity: Maxwell and Hay measure inductance, Schering measures capacitance and tan δ, Wheatstone and Kelvin measure resistance. | Forgetting the null: the bridge answer comes from the balanced arm values, not from the size of the detector reading at an arbitrary setting. | Unit slips in Maxwell and Hay: C1 must be in farads, so 0.1 µF is 0.1 × 10⁻⁶ F, and R values must be in ohms. | Using Maxwell for a high-Q coil or Hay for a low-Q coil: Maxwell suits Q from about 1 to 10, Hay suits Q above 10; Schering tan δ uses ω = 2πf. */
